// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  Zellij configuration
//
//  Deliberately minimal: it only sets what actually differs from the
//  Zellij defaults. Dumping the full default keybind table into this
//  file (as the built-in configuration plugin does when it saves) adds
//  ~250 lines that change nothing and make real customizations
//  impossible to spot. If you want to see the defaults, run:
//
//      zellij setup --dump-config
//
//  Every option below is documented at https://zellij.dev/documentation
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// UI theme. Defined in themes/ — see themes/debian.kdl for the palette
// and for a note on how the accent colour is produced.
theme "debian"

// Start plain. Load a workspace layout explicitly when you want one:
//
//     zdev
//
// Do NOT set `default_layout "dev"` unless you really want every new
// terminal to spawn a full monitoring stack (btop, lazydocker, lazysql,
// lnav...). That is roughly eight extra processes per session and it is
// the most common reason a Zellij setup "feels broken" on modest RAM.
default_layout "default"

// Session serialization writes the live layout to ~/.cache/zellij and
// resurrects it next time. It is ON by default, and it is a trap while
// you are iterating on layouts: Zellij restores the previously saved
// (possibly broken) session instead of re-reading your .kdl file, with
// dead command panes marked `start_suspended true`.
//
// Turn it back on once your layouts are stable, if you want it.
session_serialization false

// What happens when the terminal window goes away (you click the X, the
// emulator crashes, SSH drops). Zellij's default is "detach": the session
// and every process in it stay alive in the background, unreachable.
//
// That default is how you end up with runaway TUIs. A btop with no tty
// attached does not idle — it redraws in a tight loop at ~90% of a core,
// forever, and nothing on screen tells you it is happening. Seven closed
// windows once cost seven pegged cores here.
//
// "quit" makes closing the window mean what it looks like it means: the
// session dies and takes its processes with it. The trade is real —
// you lose the ability to close a terminal and pick the workspace back
// up tomorrow. Detach on purpose with Ctrl-o d when you want that;
// closing the window is then unambiguously "I am done".
on_force_close "quit"

// Note on the file picker: Zellij's shipped default pins it to `cwd "/"`,
// which drops you at the filesystem root every time. This config leaves
// the alias unset so the picker opens in the current working directory
// instead. If you prefer a fixed root, add it back explicitly:
//
// plugins {
//     filepicker location="zellij:strider" {
//         cwd "/absolute/path"
//     }
// }

// Zellij copies through the OSC 52 terminal escape sequence by default,
// which works in most modern terminals. If copying does not reach your
// system clipboard, uncomment the line matching your session type.
//
// X11:      copy_command "xclip -selection clipboard"
// Wayland:  copy_command "wl-copy"
// macOS:    copy_command "pbcopy"

// Keep the mouse usable for selection in terminals that need it.
// copy_on_select true

// Scrollback per pane. Raise it if you tail verbose logs.
// scroll_buffer_size 10000

// Uncomment if your terminal lacks powerline/nerd-font glyphs.
// simplified_ui true
